WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.069 --> 00:00:05.529
Coco is Pixar's new Day of The Dead-themed
animation and the first trailer just dropped

00:00:05.529 --> 00:00:08.690
giving us a fantastic look at some of the
characters we can look forward to meeting

00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:09.690
in the movie.

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:13.299
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here
and, in this video, I'm doing to break down

00:00:13.299 --> 00:00:17.470
the first trailer for Coco and reveal some
hidden details you might have missed.

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:22.300
The trailer opens on a scene of Miguel entering
an ofrenda, or shrine, for his musical hero

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:24.050
Ernesto de la Cruz.

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:28.410
Inside the shrine there are tons of pictures,
records and lots of DLC mentions which, is

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:33.380
of course, short for "De La Cruz", and there's
also two handmade signs that read "DLC Por

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:36.510
Siempre!" which in English means "DLC forever!".

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:40.050
There are lots of nice touches that Pixar
have put into designing this traditional Mexican

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:44.180
ofrenda, in other words, a type of altar set
up on the Day of the Dead to honour the memory

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:45.460
of a person's ancestors.

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:50.699
We can see orange and yellow marigolds, which
are the flowers traditionally used in an ofrenda.

00:00:50.699 --> 00:00:54.079
Other cool details include a plate of food
with what looks like a sweet bread or pan

00:00:54.079 --> 00:00:57.920
dulce, on top of which is a 'calavera', or
Mexican candied skull.

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:01.829
And that's because, in an ofrenda, the favourite
food of the deceased person is typically offered

00:01:01.829 --> 00:01:04.840
to make them feel welcome and comfortable.

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:09.140
Next, Miguel puts on a video cassette of some
old black and white recordings of his idol,

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:12.520
Ernesto de la Cruz, who's voiced by actor
Benjamin Bratt.

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:16.590
You might have seen Bratt on screen recently
as Jonathan Pangborn in Doctor Strange, and

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:21.450
his character in Coco is inspired by legendary
Mexican film and musical icons such as Pedro

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:24.600
Infante, Jorge Negrete and Vicente Fernández.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:28.759
OK, let's move on to the next scene where
we see Miguel unfolding a torn photo with

00:01:28.759 --> 00:01:30.869
the face of one person missing.

00:01:30.869 --> 00:01:34.750
What we already know about Coco is that Miguel
comes from a family of shoemakers, the Riveras,

00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:39.329
who banned music in the family after Miguel's
great-great-grandmother Imelda was abandoned

00:01:39.329 --> 00:01:42.619
by her husband when he left to chase his musical
dreams.

00:01:42.619 --> 00:01:47.159
This picture is probably of Miguel's great-great
grandparents and their daughter Mama Coco,

00:01:47.159 --> 00:01:51.549
who is Miguel's great-grandmother, and judging
by the broken picture frame on the floor it

00:01:51.549 --> 00:01:55.770
seems that Miguel may have chanced upon this
photo literally by accident.

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:59.909
Given this comes straight after the previous
scene of Ernesto de la Cruz on TV serenading

00:01:59.909 --> 00:02:04.570
a woman, it seems implied that the missing
character from the photo is the deceased Ernesto.

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:09.600
So, perhaps this photo is the reason why we
next see Miguel breaking into Ernesto's tomb

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:12.930
as he's trying to uncover something about
his family's past.

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:16.940
Inside the tomb, Miguel sees Ernesto's old
guitar, and as he touches it the flowers on

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:21.150
the floor light up suggesting Miguel shares
a special connection with Ernesto.

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:25.500
By the way, this moment and the way the flowers
light up and rise up around him reminds me

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:30.060
somewhat of the 2016 animated movie Kubo And
The Two Strings.

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:33.720
And this special connection is what transports
Miguel to the underworld together with his

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:38.070
dog Dante, who by the way is a Xolo, also
known as a Mexican hairless dog.

00:02:38.070 --> 00:02:41.960
Actually, the fact that the dog is named Dante
seems highly appropriate given the themes

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:46.661
of the movie, as medieval Italian poet Dante's
most famous work, The Divine Comedy, is all

00:02:46.661 --> 00:02:49.670
about the author's travels through the realms
of the dead.

00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:53.600
Finally, the trailer ends with a lovely first
look at the underworld in Coco.

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:58.170
The multi-coloured design and other-worldliness
of it reminded me a bit of Inside Out, which

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:02.440
then left me wondering whether Pixar might
include a cameo by Bing Bong here in the Land

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:03.440
of the Dead.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:07.960
Bing Bong heroically sacrificed himself to
save Joy from being trapped inside Riley's

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:12.260
Memory Dump and it would be wonderful to see
him or any other deceased Pixar characters

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:14.220
appear in Coco's underworld.

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:18.610
Talking of connections to other Pixar movies,
the inspiration for Coco actually kind of

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:21.240
came from a scene at the end of Toy Story
3.

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:26.290
Coco director Lee Unkrich, who also directed
Toy Story 3, told EW that: "At the end of

00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:31.250
Toy Story 3, we had this moment where Jessie
turns on the boom-box and the Gipsy Kings

00:03:31.250 --> 00:03:33.900
do a Spanish language version of 'You've Got
a Friend in Me.'

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:37.510
Which, it turned out, ended up being a real
precursor to this movie."

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:43.040
Obviously, there have been Day of the Dead-themed
animations before, including the 2014 film

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:47.240
The Book of Life, but I still can't wait to
see Pixar's own take on the theme.

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:51.050
So, what did you think about the first teaser
trailer for Pixar's Coco?

00:03:51.050 --> 00:03:55.360
And are there any dead Pixar characters you'd
like to see appear in Coco's Land of the Dead?

